#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Bash version gate
## Must be the first thing that runs, and is deliberately written in bash 3.2 syntax:
## everything past this point is 4.x, so an old bash would die on the next few lines
## with 'bad substitution' or 'invalid shell option name' and no idea what to do.
## Stock macOS still ships 3.2 (2007), and the BSDs ship no bash at all.
if [[ "${BASH_VERSINFO[0]}" -lt 4 ]] || { [[ "${BASH_VERSINFO[0]}" -eq 4 ]] && [[ "${BASH_VERSINFO[1]}" -lt 4 ]]; }; then
	## Plain printf, not the fEcho helpers: those are 4.x code themselves.
	printf '%s\n' "gitsby needs bash 4.4 or newer; this is bash ${BASH_VERSION}." >&2
	case "$(uname -s 2>/dev/null)" in
		Darwin)
			printf '%s\n' "  macOS still ships bash 3.2, and never replaces it. Install a current one:" >&2
			printf '%s\n' "    brew install bash        (Homebrew)" >&2
			printf '%s\n' "    sudo port install bash   (MacPorts)" >&2
			printf '%s\n' "  Both install alongside /bin/bash rather than over it, so make sure the new" >&2
			printf '%s\n' "  one comes first on your PATH." >&2 ;;
		*BSD|DragonFly)
			printf '%s\n' "  Install bash from packages or ports:" >&2
			printf '%s\n' "    pkg install bash         (FreeBSD)" >&2
			printf '%s\n' "    pkg_add bash             (OpenBSD)" >&2 ;;
		*)
			printf '%s\n' "  Install bash 4.4 or newer with your package manager." >&2 ;;
	esac
	printf '%s\n' "  Or use the PowerShell build (gitsby.ps1), which needs no bash at all." >&2
	exit 1
fi


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Settings
[[ -v doQuietly         ]] || declare -i doQuietly=0
[[ -v thisVersion       ]] || declare -r thisVersion="2.0.0"
[[ -v thisCopyrightYear ]] || declare -r thisCopyrightYear="2014-2026"
[[ -v thisAuthor        ]] || declare -r thisAuthor="Jim Collier"

## Never pop a merge-message editor mid-flow.
export GIT_MERGE_AUTOEDIT=no


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Copyright, about, & syntax (minified)
fCopyright(){ { ((doQuietly)) || ((wasShown_Copyright)); } && return; wasShown_Copyright=1;
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho_Clean "${meName} v${thisVersion}, Copyright © ${thisCopyrightYear} ${thisAuthor}."
	fEcho_Clean "Licensed under The MIT License (MIT). Full text at:"
	fEcho_Clean "  https://mit-license.org/"
	fEcho_Clean "No Warranty."
	fEcho_Clean "" ;:;}

fAbout(){ { ((doQuietly)) || ((wasShown_About)); } && return; wasShown_About=1;
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho_Clean "Safer, state-checked wrappers for everyday git. Every command verifies the"
	fEcho_Clean "repo state before acting (commit only if dirty, pull only with an upstream,"
	fEcho_Clean "push only if ahead), so each is idempotent and safe to re-run."
	fEcho_Clean "" ;:;}

fSyntax(){ { ((doQuietly)) || ((wasShown_Syntax)); } && return; wasShown_Syntax=1;
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho_Clean "Common commands:"
	fEcho_Clean "  update [msg] .......: Pull updates, then commit all local changes. Do frequently!"
	fEcho_Clean "  br create <branch> .: Create a new branch off ${mergeTargetLabel} (current work is carried or parked)."
	fEcho_Clean "  br switch [branch] .: Switch to a branch (parks current work first). No arg: back to ${mergeTargetLabel}."
	fEcho_Clean "  br [list] ..........: Fetch and list branches."
	fEcho_Clean "  status .............: Fetch and show current status."
	fEcho_Clean "One-time setup commands:"
	fEcho_Clean "  repo clone <url> ...: Clone a repo you don't have yet, into [dir] (checks out dev if it has one)."
	fEcho_Clean "  repo create <o/n> ..: Create GitHub repo 'owner/name' via gh, then connect this directory and push."
	fEcho_Clean "  repo connect [url] .: Connect this directory to an existing empty remote, and push."
	fEcho_Clean "Less common commands:"
	fEcho_Clean "  sync [msg] .........: Pull, commit, and push. Do infrequently."
	fEcho_Clean "Admin commands, e.g. for small solo projects:"
	fEcho_Clean "  br land [msg] ......: Merge current branch into ${mergeTargetLabel} (--no-ff), push, delete it local + remote."
	fEcho_Clean "  br prune ...........: Delete branches already merged into ${mergeTargetLabel}, local + remote."
	fEcho_Clean "  br hotfix <name> ...: Branch off the default branch, to correct what's already published."
	fEcho_Clean "  pr [create|n|ok n] .: Create, list, review, or accept a pull request (needs gh)."
	fEcho_Clean "  release [ver] ......: Cut a release: merge dev into main, tag, push. No ver: next after latest tag."
	fEcho_Clean "Options:"
	fEcho_Clean "  -m, --message MSG ....: Commit or merge message (or give it positionally)."
	fEcho_Clean "  -q, --quiet, -y ......: Assume yes - no prompts; if committing with no message, one is generated."
	fEcho_Clean "  --public / --private .: Visibility for the repo 'repo create' makes (default: private)."
	fEcho_Clean "  --any-identity .......: Proceed when gh's account differs from the remote's ssh key."
	fEcho_Clean "  --no-fetch ...........: Work offline: skip the pre-command fetch, and the pull."
	fEcho_Clean "  -h, --help  /  -v, --version"
	fEcho_Clean "" ;:;}


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
fMain(){

	## Generic variables
	local -i wasShown_Copyright=0; local -i wasShown_About=0; local -i wasShown_Syntax=0
	local -r mergeTargetLabel="dev/main"  ## for help text, before we know we're in a repo

	## Check for need to show help. Only the command slot counts - scanning the whole
	## argv would make a message like "add -v flag" silently short-circuit the command.
	[[ -z "${1:-}" ]] && { fCopyright; fAbout; fSyntax; return 1; }
	case "${1,,}" in
		-h|--help|help)                fCopyright; fAbout; fSyntax; return 0; ;;
		-v|--ver|--version|version)    fCopyright;                  return 0; ;;
	esac

	## Breathing room after the shell prompt (the matching trailing blank is at each exit path).
	fEcho_Clean ""

	## Validate dependencies
	fMustBeInPath git

	## Arguments
	local cmdName=""        ## positional 1: flat command, or the noun of a grouped one
	local cmdArg=""         ## positional 2: subcommand for a grouped noun, else message/branch/version/PR number
	local cmdArg2=""        ## positional 3
	local cmdArg3=""        ## positional 4: only 'repo clone <url> [dir]' goes this deep
	local commitMessage=""  ## may be set by fParseArgs (-m/--message/--msg)
	local -i doFetch=1      ## cleared by --no-fetch
	local -i remoteReachable=1  ## cleared when the pre-command fetch can't reach origin
	local repoVisibility="private"  ## --public/--private, for 'repo create'
	local -i anyIdentity=0          ## --any-identity: a gh/ssh account mismatch here is intended
	local -i isGhCommand=0          ## command goes through gh at all -> show whose account that is
	local -i isGhWrite=0            ## command WRITES through gh -> also compare against the ssh key
	local identityProbeUrl=""       ## url the ssh identity is read from (existing origin, or the one about to be set)
	local ghLoginCache=""; local sshLoginCache=""; local ghProtocolCache=""  ## per-run, each costs a round trip
	local defaultBranchCache=""; local mergeTargetCache=""  ## per-run constants, filled post-fetch
	local -i inRepo=0
	local cloneUrl=""; local cloneDir=""
	local connectMode=""; local connectUrl=""; local ghTarget=""  ## resolved by the repo create/connect validation
	local -a pruneLocal=(); local -a pruneRemote=(); local -a pruneKeep=(); local -a pruneTargetRefs=(); local pruneCurrentMerged=""  ## resolved by the br prune validation
	local -ri parseArgs_maxPositionalArgCount=4
	fParseArgs "${@}"
	cmdName="${cmdName,,}"

	## Grouped commands are '<noun> <verb> [args]'. Collapse each pair to one internal token and
	## shift the positionals down, so everything downstream still deals with a single flat name.
	## No real command has a hyphen, so rejecting one keeps the internal tokens from being typeable.
	[[ "${cmdName}" == *-* ]] && fThrowError_Usage "Unknown command '${cmdName}'. Run '${meName}' with no arguments for a list."
	case "${cmdName}" in
		repo|repository)
			case "${cmdArg,,}" in
				clone)       cmdName="repo-clone"   ;;
				create|new)  cmdName="repo-create"  ;;
				connect)     cmdName="repo-connect" ;;
				"")          fThrowError_Usage "Syntax: ${meName} repo <clone <url> [dir] | create <owner/name> | connect [url]>" ;;
				*)           fThrowError_Usage "Unknown 'repo' subcommand '${cmdArg}'. One of: clone, create, connect." ;;
			esac
			cmdArg="${cmdArg2}"; cmdArg2="${cmdArg3}"; cmdArg3=""
			;;
		br|branch)
			case "${cmdArg,,}" in
				""|list)     cmdName="br-list"   ;;
				create|new)  cmdName="br-create" ;;
				hotfix)      cmdName="br-hotfix" ;;
				switch|go)   cmdName="br-switch" ;;
				land)        cmdName="br-land"   ;;
				prune|clean) cmdName="br-prune"  ;;
				*)           fThrowError_Usage "Unknown 'br' subcommand '${cmdArg}'. One of: list, create, hotfix, switch, land, prune." ;;
			esac
			cmdArg="${cmdArg2}"; cmdArg2="${cmdArg3}"; cmdArg3=""
			;;
	esac

	## Sort commands, and route positional arg 2 (message vs branch name; -m wins for messages).
	local isMutating=1
	case "${cmdName}" in
		status|br-list)          isMutating=0 ;;
		pr)                      [[ "${cmdArg,,}" =~ ^(ok|create|new)$ ]] || isMutating=0 ;;
		update|sync|br-land)
			[[ -z "${commitMessage}" ]] && commitMessage="${cmdArg}"
			[[ -n "${cmdArg2}" ]] && fThrowError_Usage "Unexpected extra argument '${cmdArg2}'; quote your commit message."
			;;
		repo-clone) : ;;  ## the only one with a second argument of its own (the target directory)
		br-prune)
			## Takes nothing: what it deletes is decided by repo state, never by a name on the command line.
			[[ -n "${cmdArg}" ]] && fThrowError_Usage "'${meName} br prune' takes no arguments (got '${cmdArg}'); it prunes every branch already merged."
			;;
		br-create|br-hotfix|br-switch|release|repo-create|repo-connect)
			[[ -n "${cmdArg2}" ]] && fThrowError_Usage "Unexpected extra argument '${cmdArg2}'."
			;;
		*) fThrowError_Usage "Unknown command '${cmdName}'. Run '${meName}' with no arguments for a list." ;;
	esac
	[[ -n "${cmdArg3}" ]] && fThrowError_Usage "Unexpected extra argument '${cmdArg3}'."

	## No tty = nobody to answer a prompt. Read-only commands just go quiet; mutating
	## ones fail closed (require an explicit -q) so piped/cron input can't silently auto-confirm.
	if [[ ! -t 0 ]]; then
		((isMutating)) && ((! doQuietly)) && fThrowError_Usage "No terminal to confirm on; re-run with -q to proceed without prompts."
		doQuietly=1
	fi

	## pr needs gh and a valid number (except the bare list form).
	local prNum=""; local prSub=""; local prTitle=""; local prHeadBranch=""
	if [[ "${cmdName}" == "pr" ]]; then
		fMustBeInPath gh
		case "${cmdArg,,}" in
			ok)
				prSub="ok"
				prNum="${cmdArg2}"
				[[ "${prNum}" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || fThrowError_Usage "Syntax: ${meName} pr ok <number>"
				;;
			create|new)
				prSub="create"
				prTitle="${commitMessage:-${cmdArg2}}"  ## -m wins, same as everywhere else
				;;
			"") : ;;
			*)
				prNum="${cmdArg}"
				[[ "${prNum}" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || fThrowError_Usage "Syntax: ${meName} pr [create [title] | <number> | ok <number>]"
				;;
		esac
	fi

	## Every command needs a repo - except the repo ones: clone works anywhere, and create/connect
	## exist precisely to turn a plain directory into one.
	if git rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree &>/dev/null; then inRepo=1; fi
	if ((! inRepo)) && [[ "${cmdName}" != repo-* ]]; then
		fThrowError_Usage "Not inside a git repository. Change to a git project directory first."
	fi

	## Freshen remote refs so status/ahead-behind info is current. Never fatal - offline still works locally.
	## clone skips it: cwd may sit inside some unrelated repo, and the clone doesn't care about it.
	if ((doFetch)) && [[ "${cmdName}" != "repo-clone" ]] && git remote get-url origin &>/dev/null; then
		fEcho "git fetch ..."
		fpFetchRemote
	fi

	## These can't change mid-command, so resolve them once (post-fetch, so origin/HEAD is fresh).
	if ((inRepo)); then
		defaultBranchCache="$(fDefaultBranch)"
		mergeTargetCache="$(fMergeTarget)"
	fi

	## Release version resolves up front so preview and command agree (and bad input dies early).
	local releaseTag=""
	if [[ "${cmdName}" == "release" ]]; then
		releaseTag="$(fReleaseVersion)"
		## Up front, not mid-command: by the time fCmdRelease runs it has already committed and pushed.
		git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/${releaseTag}" >/dev/null && fThrowError_Usage "Tag '${releaseTag}' already exists."
	fi

	## The mutating pr subcommands check here too, so nothing can fail after the plan was confirmed.
	if [[ -n "${prSub}" ]]; then
		local prBranch=""; prBranch="$(fCurrentBranch)"
		[[ -n "${prBranch}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "Detached HEAD (no current branch); resolve that manually first."
		if [[ "${prSub}" == "ok" ]]; then
			## Where a PR lands, and whether it is a hotfix, are properties of the PR - not of
			## whatever branch you happen to be standing on. 'pr ok <n>' can be run from anywhere,
			## so ask gh which branch it proposes. Fall back to the current one if gh can't say.
			prHeadBranch="$(gh pr view "${prNum}" --json headRefName --jq .headRefName 2>/dev/null || true)"
			[[ -n "${prHeadBranch}" ]] || prHeadBranch="${prBranch}"
			## gh merges what origin already has, then deletes the branch local and remote. Work
			## that never reached origin is outside the PR and outside the merge - refuse, don't lose it.
			if [[ -n "$(git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null || true)" ]] || fIsAhead; then
				[[ "${prBranch}" == "${prHeadBranch}" ]] && fThrowError_Usage "'${prBranch}' has changes that aren't on origin, so PR #${prNum} can't include them. Run '${meName} sync' first."
				fThrowError_Usage "'${prBranch}' has changes that aren't on origin. Park them first: ${meName} sync"
			fi
		else
			[[ "${prBranch}" != "$(fMergeTarget)" && "${prBranch}" != "$(fDefaultBranch)" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "'${prBranch}' is what pull requests merge into; there is nothing to propose. Start a branch first: ${meName} br create <name>"
			## No title given: the last commit subject is what the work is called already.
			[[ -n "${prTitle}" ]] || prTitle="$(git log -1 --pretty=%s 2>/dev/null || true)"
			[[ -n "${prTitle}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "No commits on '${prBranch}' yet; nothing to propose."
			## An open PR for this branch already is the answer to 'pr create' - say so instead of letting gh error.
			local existingPr=""; existingPr="$(gh pr list --head "${prBranch}" --state open --json number --jq '.[0].number // empty' 2>/dev/null || true)"
			[[ -n "${existingPr}" ]] && fThrowError_Usage "PR #${existingPr} is already open for '${prBranch}'. View it: ${meName} pr ${existingPr}"
		fi
	fi

	## Branch arguments validate up front too, so a bad name can't survive to a nonsense preview.
	if [[ "${cmdName}" == "br-create" ]]; then
		[[ -n "${cmdArg}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "No branch name given. Syntax: ${meName} br create <new branch name>"
		git check-ref-format --branch "${cmdArg}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || fThrowError_Usage "'${cmdArg}' is not a valid branch name."
		fBranchExistsLocal  "${cmdArg}" && fThrowError_Usage "Branch '${cmdArg}' already exists; use: ${meName} br switch ${cmdArg}"
		fBranchExistsRemote "${cmdArg}" && fThrowError_Usage "Branch '${cmdArg}' already exists on origin; use: ${meName} br switch ${cmdArg}"
	elif [[ "${cmdName}" == "br-hotfix" ]]; then
		[[ -n "${cmdArg}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "No name given. Syntax: ${meName} br hotfix <name>"
		## The prefix is the marker, so put it on ourselves - and accept it if the user typed it.
		cmdArg="hotfix/${cmdArg#hotfix/}"
		git check-ref-format --branch "${cmdArg}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || fThrowError_Usage "'${cmdArg}' is not a valid branch name."
		fBranchExistsLocal  "${cmdArg}" && fThrowError_Usage "Branch '${cmdArg}' already exists; use: ${meName} br switch ${cmdArg}"
		fBranchExistsRemote "${cmdArg}" && fThrowError_Usage "Branch '${cmdArg}' already exists on origin; use: ${meName} br switch ${cmdArg}"
	elif [[ "${cmdName}" == "br-switch" ]]; then
		[[ -z "${cmdArg}" ]] || fBranchExistsLocal "${cmdArg}" || fBranchExistsRemote "${cmdArg}" || fThrowError_Usage "No branch '${cmdArg}' locally or on origin. To create it: ${meName} br create ${cmdArg}"
		## Refusing a dirty protected branch belongs here too, before the plan is shown and confirmed.
		local switchTarget="${cmdArg}"
		[[ -n "${switchTarget}" ]] || switchTarget="$(fMergeTarget)"
		if [[ "$(fCurrentBranch)" != "${switchTarget}" ]] && fIsProtectedBranch && [[ -n "$(git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null || true)" ]]; then
			fThrowError_Usage "Working tree has changes on '$(fCurrentBranch)'; won't auto-commit to a protected branch. Carry them to a new branch (${meName} br create <name>), or commit them here deliberately (${meName} update) first."
		fi
	fi

	## br prune: work out what goes before anything is shown, so the plan names every branch by name.
	if [[ "${cmdName}" == "br-prune" ]]; then
		[[ -n "$(fCurrentBranch)" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "Detached HEAD (no current branch); resolve that manually first."
		fpResolvePrune
		if ((! ${#pruneLocal[@]})) && ((! ${#pruneRemote[@]})); then
			## "no branch is merged" would be a lie when the merged one is the branch we're standing on.
			if [[ -n "${pruneCurrentMerged}" ]]; then
				fEcho "Nothing to prune from here."
				fEcho_Clean "  Current branch '${pruneCurrentMerged}' is merged, but you're on it; switch off it to prune it."
			else
				fEcho "Nothing to prune; no branch is fully merged into '$(fMergeTarget)' yet."
			fi
			((${#pruneKeep[@]})) && fEcho_Clean "  Keeping (not merged yet): $(fJoin "${pruneKeep[@]}")"
			fEcho_Clean ""
			return 0
		fi
	fi

	## repo clone: derive the target dir, and make re-runs a no-op instead of an error.
	if [[ "${cmdName}" == "repo-clone" ]]; then
		[[ -n "${cmdArg}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "No URL given. Syntax: ${meName} repo clone <url> [directory]"
		cloneUrl="${cmdArg}"
		cloneDir="${cmdArg2}"
		if [[ -z "${cloneDir}" ]]; then
			cloneDir="${cloneUrl%/}"; cloneDir="${cloneDir%.git}"; cloneDir="${cloneDir##*/}"; cloneDir="${cloneDir##*:}"
			[[ -n "${cloneDir}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "Can't derive a directory name from '${cloneUrl}'; give one explicitly."
		fi
		if [[ -e "${cloneDir}" ]]; then
			local existingUrl=""
			existingUrl="$(git -C "${cloneDir}" remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || true)"
			if [[ -e "${cloneDir}/.git" && "${existingUrl}" == "${cloneUrl}" ]]; then
				fEcho "'${cloneDir}' is already a clone of that URL; nothing to do."
				fEcho_Clean ""
				return 0
			fi
			## An empty dir is fine (git allows it); anything else would clobber.
			[[ -d "${cloneDir}" && -z "$(ls -A "${cloneDir}" 2>/dev/null)" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "'${cloneDir}' already exists and isn't a clone of that URL."
		fi
	fi

	## repo create/connect: resolve what we're publishing to before the preview, so the plan is real.
	## Same machinery, one difference - only 'create' will bring a remote into existence.
	if [[ "${cmdName}" == "repo-create" || "${cmdName}" == "repo-connect" ]]; then
		local -i wantCreate=0; [[ "${cmdName}" == "repo-create" ]] && wantCreate=1
		local -i hasWork=0
		if ((inRepo)); then
			if git rev-parse -q --verify HEAD &>/dev/null || [[ -n "$(git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null || true)" ]]; then hasWork=1; fi
		elif [[ -n "$(ls -A 2>/dev/null)" ]]; then
			hasWork=1
		fi
		((hasWork)) || fThrowError_Usage "Nothing to publish here: no commits and no files."
		local originUrl=""
		((inRepo)) && originUrl="$(git remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || true)"
		if [[ -n "${originUrl}" ]]; then
			((wantCreate)) && fThrowError_Usage "origin is already set to '$(fMaskUrl "${originUrl}")', so there is no repo left to create. Push what you have with: ${meName} repo connect"
			[[ -z "${cmdArg}" || "${cmdArg}" == "${originUrl}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "origin is already set to '$(fMaskUrl "${originUrl}")'; changing remotes is raw-git territory."
			connectMode="push"; connectUrl="${originUrl}"
		elif ((wantCreate)); then
			[[ -n "${cmdArg}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "No target given. Syntax: ${meName} repo create <owner/name>"
			[[ "${cmdArg}" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$ && ! -e "${cmdArg}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "'${cmdArg}' isn't a GitHub 'owner/name'; only GitHub repos can be created from here. For a remote that already exists: ${meName} repo connect ${cmdArg}"
			fMustBeInPath gh
			ghTarget="${cmdArg}"
			case "$(gh repo view "${ghTarget}" --json isEmpty --jq .isEmpty 2>/dev/null || true)" in
				"")     connectMode="create" ;;
				true)   fThrowError_Usage "github.com/${ghTarget} already exists and is empty; connect to it instead: ${meName} repo connect ${ghTarget}" ;;
				*)      fThrowError_Usage "github.com/${ghTarget} already has commits; clone it instead (${meName} repo clone), or reconcile with raw git." ;;
			esac
		else
			[[ -n "${cmdArg}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "No remote configured and no target given. Syntax: ${meName} repo connect <url | owner/name>"
			if [[ "${cmdArg}" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$ && ! -e "${cmdArg}" ]]; then
				## owner/name shorthand: gh can say whether it exists and whether it's empty.
				fMustBeInPath gh
				ghTarget="${cmdArg}"
				local isEmpty=""
				isEmpty="$(gh repo view "${ghTarget}" --json isEmpty --jq .isEmpty 2>/dev/null || true)"
				if [[ -z "${isEmpty}" ]]; then
					fThrowError_Usage "github.com/${ghTarget} doesn't exist, or you can't see it. To create it: ${meName} repo create ${ghTarget}"
				elif [[ "${isEmpty}" == "true" ]]; then
					## gh never uses a host alias, so this is the canonical url - same one gh itself would build.
					if [[ "$(fpGhProtocol)" == "ssh" ]]; then connectUrl="git@github.com:${ghTarget}.git"; else connectUrl="https://github.com/${ghTarget}.git"; fi
					connectMode="add"
				else
					fThrowError_Usage "github.com/${ghTarget} already has commits; clone it instead (${meName} repo clone), or reconcile with raw git."
				fi
			else
				case "$(fpProbeRemote "${cmdArg}")" in
					missing)  fThrowError_Usage "Can't reach '$(fMaskUrl "${cmdArg}")' (doesn't exist, or no access). Create it first, or on GitHub: ${meName} repo create <owner/name>" ;;
					nonempty) fThrowError_Usage "'$(fMaskUrl "${cmdArg}")' already has history; clone it instead (${meName} repo clone ${cmdArg}), or reconcile with raw git." ;;
					empty)    connectMode="add"; connectUrl="${cmdArg}" ;;
				esac
			fi
		fi
	fi

	## Which commands go through gh, which of those WRITE through it, and which url the ssh
	## identity should be read from. For pr that's the origin we already have. repo create and
	## connect have no origin yet - but the one they are about to set is knowable, because gh
	## never uses a host alias: it builds 'git@github.com:owner/name.git' from its own protocol
	## setting. So the identity that repo will live with afterward can be checked before we start.
	case "${cmdName}" in
		pr)
			isGhCommand=1
			if [[ "${prSub}" == "create" || "${prSub}" == "ok" ]]; then
				isGhWrite=1; identityProbeUrl="$(git remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || true)"
			fi
			;;
		repo-create)
			isGhCommand=1; isGhWrite=1
			[[ "$(fpGhProtocol)" == "ssh" ]] && identityProbeUrl="git@github.com:${ghTarget}.git"
			;;
		repo-connect)
			if [[ -n "${ghTarget}" ]]; then
				isGhCommand=1; isGhWrite=1
				## connectUrl is the url we resolved ourselves, so probe that rather than guess.
				[[ "${connectUrl}" == *@*:* ]] && identityProbeUrl="${connectUrl}"
			fi
			;;
	esac

	## A gh write acting as a different account than the key git pushes with is a wrong-account
	## mistake waiting to happen, and it is outward-facing. Refuse it unattended (nobody is there
	## to read a warning); warn interactively, right before the prompt. --any-identity means
	## the difference is intended.
	local identityMismatch=""
	if ((isGhWrite)) && ((! anyIdentity)); then
		identityMismatch="$(fpIdentityMismatch "$(fpGhLogin)" "$(fpSshLogin "${identityProbeUrl}")")"
		## Up front, like every other refusal: don't show a plan we won't run.
		[[ -n "${identityMismatch}" ]] && ((doQuietly)) && fThrowError_Usage "${identityMismatch} Nothing was done. Re-run with --any-identity if that is intended."
	fi

	## Read-only commands
	if ((! isMutating)); then
		case "${cmdName}" in
			status)  fpShowStatus 1 ;;
			br-list) fEcho "git branch -a -vv"; git branch -a -vv; fEcho_ResetBlankCounter ;;
			pr)      fCmdPrView "${prNum}" ;;
		esac
		fEcho_Clean ""
		return 0
	fi

	## Mutating commands: show state and plan, confirm, execute, show state again.
	## clone, and create/connect from a plain dir, have no repo state to show; a smaller header stands in.
	if [[ "${cmdName}" == "repo-clone" ]]; then
		fEcho_Clean ""
		fEcho_Clean "Directory ....: ${PWD}"
		fEcho_Clean "Remote .......: $(fMaskUrl "${cloneUrl}")"
		fpShowIdentity "${cloneUrl}"
		fEcho_Clean "Clone into ...: ${cloneDir}"
	elif ((! inRepo)); then
		local remoteDisp="${connectUrl}"
		[[ -n "${remoteDisp}" ]] && remoteDisp="$(fMaskUrl "${remoteDisp}")" || remoteDisp="github.com/${ghTarget} (to be created)"
		fEcho_Clean ""
		fEcho_Clean "Directory ....: ${PWD}"
		fEcho_Clean "Remote .......: ${remoteDisp}"
		fpShowIdentity "${connectUrl}"
		fEcho_Clean "Branch .......: (not a git repository yet)"
	else
		[[ -n "$(fCurrentBranch)" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "Detached HEAD (no current branch); resolve that manually first."
		fpShowStatus 1
	fi
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho_Clean "Going to do (steps marked * only if needed, based on repo state):"
	fpPreview "${cmdName}"
	## Last thing before the prompt, so it can't scroll away above the plan.
	if [[ -n "${identityMismatch}" ]]; then
		fEcho_Clean ""
		fEcho "*** WRONG ACCOUNT? ***"
		fEcho_Clean "  ${identityMismatch}"
		fEcho_Clean "  gh does the pull request work, so it happens as '$(fpGhLogin)'."
		fEcho_Clean "  Continue only if that is what you mean. (--any-identity silences this.)"
	fi
	if ((! doQuietly)); then
		fEcho_Clean ""
		fPromptYN "Continue? (y|n): " || { fEcho "User aborted."; return 1; }
	fi

	case "${cmdName}" in
		update)                   fCmdCommitPull ;;
		sync)                     fCmdPush ;;
		br-create)                fCmdNewBranch "${cmdArg}" ;;
		br-hotfix)                fCmdHotfix "${cmdArg}" ;;
		br-switch)                fCmdGoBranch "${cmdArg}" ;;
		br-land)                  fCmdLand ;;
		br-prune)                 fCmdPrune ;;
		pr)                       if [[ "${prSub}" == "create" ]]; then fCmdPrCreate "${prTitle}"; else fCmdPrAccept "${prNum}"; fi ;;
		release)                  fCmdRelease ;;
		repo-clone)               fCmdClone ;;
		repo-create|repo-connect) fCmdConnect ;;
	esac

	fEcho_Clean ""
	if [[ "${cmdName}" == "repo-clone" ]]; then
		fEcho "Cloned into '${cloneDir}'."  ## the after-status would show the wrong (current) directory
	else
		fpShowStatus
	fi
	fEcho ""
	fEcho "Done."
	fEcho_Clean ""
:;}


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Commands

fCmdCommit(){
	## Never stage a conflicted tree. 'git add --all' marks a conflicted file resolved, so the
	## markers themselves would be committed, and then pushed by sync. Ordinary use reaches this:
	## a pull whose autostash reapply conflicts still exits 0, so nothing upstream of here notices.
	local -a conflicted=()
	mapfile -t conflicted < <(git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U 2>/dev/null || true)
	if ((${#conflicted[@]})); then
		fEcho_Clean ""
		fEcho "Unresolved conflicts; nothing was committed:"
		fpCappedList printf '%s\n' "${conflicted[@]}"
		fThrowError_Usage "Resolve those, then run '${meName} update' again. Git also kept your pre-pull tree - see 'git stash list'."
	fi
	fRun git add --all
	if [[ -z "$(git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null || true)" ]]; then
		fEcho "Nothing to commit."
	elif [[ -n "${commitMessage}" ]]; then
		fRun git commit -m "${commitMessage}"
	elif ((doQuietly)); then
		fRun git commit -m "${meName} ${serialDT}"  ## quiet mode can't open an editor
	else
		fRun git commit
	fi
:;}

fCmdPull(){
	## --autostash instead of a manual stash push/pop: a failed pull (diverged, offline)
	## leaves the tree intact instead of stranding work in the stash.
	## Skipping beats failing when the remote is simply out of reach: update is the only
	## way to commit, so being offline must not turn a good commit into a failed command.
	## A reachable remote that can't fast-forward is a real problem and still fails hard.
	if ((! doFetch)); then
		fEcho "Skipping the pull (--no-fetch)."
	elif ((! remoteReachable)); then
		fEcho "WARNING: remote unreachable; skipping the pull. Local changes still get committed."
	elif fHasUpstream; then
		fRun git pull --ff-only --autostash
	else
		fEcho "No upstream configured for this branch; nothing to pull."
	fi
:;}

fPullIfOnline(){
	## The pull inside a multi-step command. Same offline rule as fCmdPull, quietly: --no-fetch
	## and an unreachable remote both mean skip. Extra arguments go through to git pull.
	((doFetch)) && ((remoteReachable)) && fHasUpstream && fRun git pull --ff-only "${@}"
:;}

fCmdCommitPull(){
	## Pull BEFORE committing. Committing first mints a local commit, so a remote that merely
	## moved ahead is now diverged and --ff-only refuses - which is the everyday case, not an
	## edge one. Pulling first fast-forwards (the dirty tree rides over on --autostash) and the
	## commit lands on top, so history stays linear and --ff-only stays satisfiable.
	fCmdPull
	fCmdCommit
:;}

fCmdPush(){
	fCmdCommitPull
	if ! git remote get-url origin &>/dev/null; then
		fEcho "No 'origin' remote; nothing to push."
	elif ! fHasUpstream; then
		fRun git push -u origin HEAD  ## first publish of this branch
	elif fIsAhead; then
		fRun git push
	else
		fEcho "Nothing to push."
	fi
:;}

fCmdNewBranch(){
	## Branch-name validation already happened up front in fMain.
	local -r newBranch="${1:-}"
	local -r baseBranch="$(fMergeTarget)"
	if fIsProtectedBranch; then
		## Don't commit WIP to main/dev; a dirty tree survives checkout -b, so carry it to the new branch.
		if [[ "$(fCurrentBranch)" != "${baseBranch}" ]]; then
			fRun git checkout "${baseBranch}"
		fi
		fPullIfOnline --autostash
	else
		fCmdPush  ## park current work safely first
		fRun git checkout "${baseBranch}"
		fPullIfOnline
	fi
	fRun git checkout -b "${newBranch}"
	if git remote get-url origin &>/dev/null; then
		fRun git push -u origin "${newBranch}"
	fi
:;}

fCmdGoBranch(){
	local targetBranch="${1:-}"
	[[ -n "${targetBranch}" ]] || targetBranch="$(fMergeTarget)"
	if [[ "$(fCurrentBranch)" == "${targetBranch}" ]]; then
		fEcho "Already on '${targetBranch}'."
		fPullIfOnline
		return 0
	fi
	## The dirty-protected-branch refusal happens up front in fMain, before the plan is shown.
	fCmdPush  ## park current work safely first
	fRun git checkout "${targetBranch}"  ## auto-creates a tracking branch if it only exists on origin
	fPullIfOnline
:;}

fCmdHotfix(){
	## A branch off the default branch, for correcting what is already published. Feature work
	## still goes through dev; this exists because the default branch is what the world reads.
	local -r newBranch="${1:-}"
	local -r baseBranch="$(fDefaultBranch)"
	if fIsProtectedBranch; then
		## Same rule as br create: don't commit work-in-progress to a protected branch, carry it.
		if [[ "$(fCurrentBranch)" != "${baseBranch}" ]]; then
			fRun git checkout "${baseBranch}"
		fi
		fPullIfOnline --autostash
	else
		fCmdPush  ## park current work safely first
		fRun git checkout "${baseBranch}"
		fPullIfOnline
	fi
	fRun git checkout -b "${newBranch}"
	if git remote get-url origin &>/dev/null; then
		fRun git push -u origin "${newBranch}"
	fi
:;}

fCmdLand(){
	## Merges the current branch into dev (or main/master) - backwards from 'git merge', but saves a step.
	local -r workBranch="$(fCurrentBranch)"
	local -r targetBranch="$(fBranchTarget "${workBranch}")"
	[[ "${workBranch}" != "${targetBranch}" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "Already on '${targetBranch}'. Run this from the branch to merge in: ${meName} br switch <branch>, then ${meName} br land"
	[[ "${workBranch}" != "$(fDefaultBranch)" ]] || fThrowError_Usage "'${workBranch}' is the default branch; landing it on '${targetBranch}' is backwards. To cut a release: ${meName} release"
	local mergeMessage="${commitMessage}"
	[[ -n "${mergeMessage}" ]] || mergeMessage="Merge ${workBranch}"
	local -ri wasHotfix="$(fIsHotfixBranch "${workBranch}" && echo 1 || echo 0)"
	fCmdPush
	## After the push, not before: the warning reads the branch tip, and uncommitted work
	## only becomes part of it here. Checking first missed a hotfix whose bin/ edit was
	## still in the working tree - the ordinary way of doing one.
	((wasHotfix)) && fpWarnHotfixTouchedBin "${workBranch}" "${targetBranch}"
	fRun git checkout "${targetBranch}"
	fPullIfOnline
	fRun git merge --no-ff "${workBranch}" -m "${mergeMessage}"
	## The merge must reach origin before the remote work branch goes away, or origin
	## loses its only ref to those commits. Publish an upstream-less target first.
	if git remote get-url origin &>/dev/null; then
		if fHasUpstream; then fRun git push; else fRun git push -u origin HEAD; fi
	fi
	fRun git branch -d "${workBranch}"
	if fBranchExistsRemote "${workBranch}"; then
		## Non-fatal: someone (a PR merge, another clone) may have deleted it already.
		fEcho_Clean ""
		fEcho "git push origin --delete ${workBranch} ..."
		git push origin --delete "${workBranch}" || fEcho "WARNING: couldn't delete the remote branch (already gone?); continuing."
		fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
	fi
	fPullIfOnline
	## The hotfix now has to reach dev too, or the next release undoes it.
	((wasHotfix)) && fpBackMergeToDev
:;}

fCmdPrune(){
	## Deletes exactly what the plan listed - fpResolvePrune did all the deciding, up front.
	local -i doneLocal=0; local -i doneRemote=0
	local branch=""; local reKept=" "
	for branch in "${pruneLocal[@]}"; do
		## -D with our own gate, not -d. 'git branch -d' asks whether the branch is contained in
		## its upstream, or in HEAD when it has none - neither of which is the question here, and
		## the second one refuses a genuinely-merged local-only branch from any other branch.
		## Re-checked right now rather than trusting the plan: the prompt may have sat a while.
		if ! fIsMergedInto "refs/heads/${branch}" "${pruneTargetRefs[@]}"; then
			fEcho "'${branch}' is no longer contained in $(fMergeTarget); leaving it alone."
			reKept="${reKept}${branch} "
			continue
		fi
		fRun git branch -D "${branch}"
		doneLocal=$((doneLocal + 1))
	done
	for branch in "${pruneRemote[@]}"; do
		## "Leaving it alone" has to mean the remote copy too, or the message is a lie.
		[[ "${reKept}" == *" ${branch} "* ]] && continue
		## Non-fatal, same as br land: someone else may have deleted it already.
		fEcho_Clean ""
		fEcho "git push origin --delete ${branch} ..."
		if git push origin --delete "${branch}"; then
			doneRemote=$((doneRemote + 1))
		else
			fEcho "WARNING: couldn't delete origin/${branch} (already gone?); continuing."
		fi
		fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
	done
	## Close with the count, so a wall of git output still ends in a plain answer.
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho "Pruned ${doneLocal} local, ${doneRemote} on origin."
	[[ -n "${pruneCurrentMerged}" ]] && fEcho "Kept '${pruneCurrentMerged}' - merged, but it's the current branch."
	((${#pruneKeep[@]})) && fEcho "Kept $(fJoin "${pruneKeep[@]}") - not merged into $(fMergeTarget) yet."
:;}

fCmdClone(){
	fRun git clone "${cloneUrl}" "${cloneDir}"
	## Opinionated: if the repo works dev-first, start there.
	if git -C "${cloneDir}" show-ref --verify --quiet refs/remotes/origin/dev; then
		fRun git -C "${cloneDir}" checkout dev
	fi
:;}

## Serves both 'repo create' and 'repo connect'; connectMode is what they disagree about.
fCmdConnect(){
	((inRepo)) || fRun git init -b main
	fCmdCommit  ## publish everything as-is; no-op when clean
	case "${connectMode}" in
		create)
			fRun gh repo create "${ghTarget}" "--${repoVisibility}" --source . --push --remote origin
			;;
		add)
			fRun git remote add origin "${connectUrl}"
			fRun git push -u origin HEAD
			;;
		push)
			## origin already set - just make sure everything is published
			if ! fHasUpstream; then
				fRun git push -u origin HEAD
			elif fIsAhead; then
				fRun git push
			else
				fEcho "Nothing to push; already connected and current."
			fi
			;;
	esac
:;}

fCmdPrView(){
	## Bare: list open PRs. With a number: view it plus its diff.
	local -r prNum="${1:-}"
	if [[ -z "${prNum}" ]]; then
		fRun gh pr list
	else
		fRun gh pr view "${prNum}"
		fRun gh pr diff "${prNum}"
	fi
:;}

fCmdPrCreate(){
	## GitHub can only diff what origin has, so park the work first - same as br land and release do.
	local -r title="${1:-}"
	fCmdPush
	fRun gh pr create --base "$(fBranchTarget)" --head "$(fCurrentBranch)" --title "${title}" --body ""
:;}

fCmdPrAccept(){
	local -r prNum="${1:-}"
	## Resolve both before gh deletes the branch out from under us, and off the PR's own head
	## branch (resolved up front) rather than the current one - they need not be the same.
	local -r prTargetBranch="$(fBranchTarget "${prHeadBranch}")"
	local -ri wasHotfixPr="$(fIsHotfixBranch "${prHeadBranch}" && echo 1 || echo 0)"
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho "gh pr review ${prNum} --approve ..."
	## Best-effort: gh refuses to approve your own PR; merging is the part that matters.
	gh pr review "${prNum}" --approve || fEcho "Could not approve (own PR?); merging anyway."
	fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
	fRun gh pr merge "${prNum}" --merge --delete-branch
	## gh deletes the PR's branch on the remote but leaves our origin/* copy behind, so an
	## upstream still looks present. Prune first; if ours is the branch that just went away,
	## pulling it can only fail - land on the merge target instead.
	fpFetchRemote
	fHasUpstream || fRun git checkout "${prTargetBranch}"
	fPullIfOnline
	## Same rule as land: a hotfix that reached the default branch still owes dev a merge.
	((wasHotfixPr)) && fpBackMergeToDev
:;}

fCmdRelease(){
	## Cuts a release: merge dev into main/master --no-ff (if the repo has a dev), tag, push both.
	local -r mainBranch="$(fDefaultBranch)"
	local devBranch=""
	{ fBranchExistsLocal dev || fBranchExistsRemote dev; } && devBranch="dev"
	local -r startBranch="$(fCurrentBranch)"
	fCmdPush  ## park current work safely first
	if [[ -n "${devBranch}" && "$(fCurrentBranch)" != "${devBranch}" ]]; then
		fRun git checkout "${devBranch}"  ## freshen dev so the release has all of it
		fPullIfOnline
	fi
	if [[ "$(fCurrentBranch)" != "${mainBranch}" ]]; then
		fRun git checkout "${mainBranch}"
	fi
	fPullIfOnline
	if [[ -n "${devBranch}" ]]; then
		local mergeMessage="${commitMessage}"
		[[ -n "${mergeMessage}" ]] || mergeMessage="Release ${releaseTag}"
		fRun git merge --no-ff "${devBranch}" -m "${mergeMessage}"
	fi
	fRun git tag -a "${releaseTag}" -m "${releaseTag}"
	## The branch has to reach origin, not just the tag - otherwise origin gets the commits
	## as tag payload while its main still points at the old release. Same trap as land's.
	if git remote get-url origin &>/dev/null; then
		if fHasUpstream; then fRun git push; else fRun git push -u origin HEAD; fi
		fRun git push origin "${releaseTag}"
	fi
	## Fast-forward dev to include the release merge and tag, so dev isn't left a commit behind.
	## ff-only (not branch -f): if dev moved mid-release, skip rather than discard work.
	if [[ -n "${devBranch}" ]]; then
		if git merge-base --is-ancestor "${devBranch}" "${mainBranch}"; then
			fRun git checkout "${devBranch}"
			fRun git merge --ff-only "${mainBranch}"
			fHasUpstream && fRun git push
		else
			fEcho "WARNING: '${devBranch}' gained commits during the release; leaving it as-is."
		fi
	fi
	## Don't leave the user parked on main.
	if [[ -n "${startBranch}" && "${startBranch}" != "$(fCurrentBranch)" && "${startBranch}" != "${mainBranch}" ]]; then
		fRun git checkout "${startBranch}"
	fi
:;}


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Git-state helpers (each verifies, assumes nothing)

fCurrentBranch(){      git branch --show-current 2>/dev/null || true ;:;}
fHasUpstream(){        git rev-parse --abbrev-ref '@{u}' &>/dev/null ;}
fIsAhead(){            [[ -n "$(git rev-list -n 1 '@{u}..' 2>/dev/null || true)" ]] ;}  ## -n 1: stop at the first commit; the count doesn't matter
fBranchExistsLocal(){  git show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/${1:-}" ;}
fBranchExistsRemote(){ git show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/remotes/origin/${1:-}" ;}

fIsProtectedBranch(){
	## main/master/dev: branches WIP should never be auto-committed to, or deleted.
	## Takes a branch name; no argument means the current one.
	local -r cur="${1:-$(fCurrentBranch)}"
	[[ "${cur}" == "$(fDefaultBranch)" ]] && return 0
	[[ "${cur}" == "main" || "${cur}" == "master" ]] && return 0  ## a leftover one isn't ours to touch either
	[[ "${cur}" == "dev" ]] && { fBranchExistsLocal dev || fBranchExistsRemote dev; }
}

fIsMergedInto(){
	## True when ref $1 is already contained in any of the refs after it.
	local -r ref="${1}"; shift
	local into=""
	for into in "${@}"; do
		git rev-parse -q --verify "${into}" >/dev/null || continue
		git merge-base --is-ancestor "${ref}" "${into}" 2>/dev/null && return 0
	done
	return 1
}

fJoin(){
	## Comma-separated list, for one-line reports.
	local out=""; local item=""
	for item in "${@}"; do out="${out:+${out}, }${item}"; done
	echo "${out}"
:;}

fpResolvePrune(){
	## Sorts local branches into what's already landed and what isn't. Ancestry is an exact
	## test here only because gitsby always lands with a real merge commit - a squash- or
	## rebase-landed branch never looks contained, and is kept rather than guessed at.
	local -r target="$(fMergeTarget)"
	local -r targetRemoteRef="refs/remotes/origin/${target}"
	fBranchExistsLocal  "${target}" && pruneTargetRefs+=("refs/heads/${target}")
	fBranchExistsRemote "${target}" && pruneTargetRefs+=("${targetRemoteRef}")
	local -r currentBranch="$(fCurrentBranch)"
	local branch=""
	while read -r branch; do
		[[ -n "${branch}" ]] || continue
		## The branch we're standing on can't be deleted, and protected ones never are.
		## But if it WOULD have qualified, say so - otherwise it just vanishes from every list.
		if [[ "${branch}" == "${currentBranch}" ]]; then
			if ! fIsProtectedBranch "${branch}" && ((${#pruneTargetRefs[@]})) && fIsMergedInto "refs/heads/${branch}" "${pruneTargetRefs[@]}"; then
				pruneCurrentMerged="${branch}"
			fi
			continue
		fi
		fIsProtectedBranch "${branch}" && continue
		if ((${#pruneTargetRefs[@]})) && fIsMergedInto "refs/heads/${branch}" "${pruneTargetRefs[@]}"; then
			pruneLocal+=("${branch}")
			## The remote copy goes only when origin has the merge too: a landing that hasn't
			## been pushed yet leaves origin holding the only ref to that work.
			if fBranchExistsRemote "${branch}" && fIsMergedInto "refs/remotes/origin/${branch}" "${targetRemoteRef}"; then
				pruneRemote+=("${branch}")
			fi
		else
			pruneKeep+=("${branch}")
		fi
	done < <(git for-each-ref --format='%(refname:short)' refs/heads/ 2>/dev/null || true)
:;}

fBranchSync(){
	## Ahead/behind for the branch line; empty when in sync, so a quiet line means "nothing pending".
	fHasUpstream || { echo "(no upstream)"; return 0; }
	local -i behind=0; local -i ahead=0
	read -r behind ahead < <(git rev-list --left-right --count '@{u}...HEAD' 2>/dev/null || echo "0 0")
	local out=""
	((ahead))  && out="ahead ${ahead}"
	((behind)) && { [[ -n "${out}" ]] && out="${out}, "; out="${out}behind ${behind}"; }
	[[ -n "${out}" ]] && echo "[${out}]"
:;}

fCommitIdentity(){
	## What actually gets stamped on commits (config or GIT_AUTHOR_*) - and shown to everyone on the remote.
	local ident=""
	ident="$(git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT 2>/dev/null || true)"
	ident="${ident% * *}"  ## drop the trailing "timestamp timezone"
	echo "${ident:-(unset)}"
:;}

fMaskUrl(){
	## Hide userinfo in displayed URLs (https://user:token@host -> https://***@host);
	## a credentialed origin would otherwise echo the token on every run.
	local url="${1:-}"
	if [[ "${url}" =~ ^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*://)[^/@]+@(.*)$ ]]; then
		url="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}***@${BASH_REMATCH[2]}"
	fi
	echo "${url}"
:;}

fSshTarget(){
	## Host to ask ssh about, pulled out of a remote URL. Empty for https and local-path remotes.
	local url="${1:-}"
	case "${url}" in
		ssh://*) url="${url#ssh://}"; url="${url%%/*}"; url="${url##*@}"; echo "${url%%:*}" ;;
		*://*)   : ;;                                    ## https/git/file: no ssh identity involved
		*@*:*)   url="${url%%:*}"; echo "${url##*@}" ;;   ## scp-like: user@host:path
		?*:*)    echo "${url%%:*}" ;;                    ## scp-like without a user
	esac
:;}

fSshConnectTarget(){
	## '[user@]host' to actually connect to, so an explicit user in the remote URL is honored.
	## fSshTarget deliberately returns the bare host - that's what 'ssh -G' wants for display.
	local -r url="${1:-}"
	local -r host="$(fSshTarget "${url}")"
	[[ -n "${host}" ]] || return 0
	local user=""
	case "${url}" in
		ssh://*@*) user="${url#ssh://}"; user="${user%%@*}" ;;
		*://*)     : ;;
		*@*:*)     user="${url%%@*}" ;;
	esac
	[[ -n "${user}" ]] && echo "${user}@${host}" || echo "${host}"
:;}

fpGhLogin(){
	## The account gh's token belongs to. gh talks to the API over https and never consults
	## ssh config, so this is who every gh-backed command acts as - regardless of which key
	## git pushes with. '?' when gh can't say (missing, logged out, offline).
	if [[ -z "${ghLoginCache:-}" ]]; then
		ghLoginCache="?"
		if command -v gh &>/dev/null; then
			local login=""; login="$(GH_PROMPT_DISABLED=1 gh api user --jq .login 2>/dev/null || true)"
			[[ -n "${login}" ]] && ghLoginCache="${login}"
		fi
	fi
	echo "${ghLoginCache}"
:;}

fpGhProtocol(){
	## Which transport gh hands to git for github.com ('ssh' or 'https'). Host-specific, not the
	## global default - they can disagree, and the host one is what github.com operations use.
	if [[ -z "${ghProtocolCache:-}" ]]; then
		ghProtocolCache="https"
		if command -v gh &>/dev/null; then
			local proto=""; proto="$(gh config get -h github.com git_protocol 2>/dev/null || true)"
			[[ "${proto}" == "ssh" ]] && ghProtocolCache="ssh"
		fi
	fi
	echo "${ghProtocolCache}"
:;}

fpSshLogin(){
	## The account this remote's ssh key authenticates as. GitHub answers 'Hi <user>!' and always
	## exits 1, so parse the greeting, not the status. BatchMode + a connect timeout: never prompt,
	## never hang. '?' for https/local remotes, no agent, or anything else unresolvable - and a
	## deploy key answers with a repo name, which simply won't match any login. Unknown is not wrong.
	local -r target="$(fSshConnectTarget "${1:-}")"
	if [[ -z "${sshLoginCache:-}" ]]; then
		sshLoginCache="?"
		if [[ -n "${target}" ]] && command -v ssh &>/dev/null; then
			local greeting=""
			greeting="$(ssh -T -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=3 -- "${target}" 2>&1 || true)"
			## Per line, because the greeting is not necessarily the first one: we capture stderr
			## too, and ssh puts 'Warning: Permanently added ...' (StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new)
			## or 'Warning: Identity file ... not accessible' ahead of it. Anchoring to the whole
			## string silently answered '?' for exactly the multi-key setups this is meant to catch.
			local greetingLine=""
			while IFS= read -r greetingLine; do
				[[ "${greetingLine}" =~ ^Hi\ ([A-Za-z0-9_.:/-]+)! ]] || continue
				sshLoginCache="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"; break
			done <<< "${greeting}"
		fi
	fi
	echo "${sshLoginCache}"
:;}

fpIdentityMismatch(){
	## Prints why the two identities disagree, or nothing. Only a mismatch both sides KNOW about
	## counts: '?' on either side means we couldn't tell, which is not the same as being wrong.
	local -r ghLogin="${1:-?}"; local -r sshLogin="${2:-?}"
	[[ "${ghLogin}" == "?" || "${sshLogin}" == "?" ]] && return 0
	[[ "${ghLogin}" == "${sshLogin}" ]] && return 0
	echo "gh acts as '${ghLogin}', but this remote's key authenticates as '${sshLogin}'."
:;}

fpProbeRemote(){
	## One network round-trip: does the remote exist, and does it have history?
	## Prints missing | empty | nonempty. No auth prompts - a bad https URL would
	## otherwise stop and ask for credentials mid-run.
	local -r url="${1:-}"
	local -a probeEnv=(GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0)
	[[ -z "${GIT_SSH_COMMAND:-}" ]] && probeEnv+=(GIT_SSH_COMMAND="ssh -o ConnectTimeout=3")
	local out=""; local -i rc=0
	out="$(env "${probeEnv[@]}" git ls-remote "${url}" 2>/dev/null)" || rc=$?
	if ((rc)); then echo "missing"
	elif [[ -n "${out}" ]]; then echo "nonempty"
	else echo "empty"
	fi
:;}

fpFetchRemote(){
	## Fetch with --prune (stale origin/* refs would fool the existence checks) and heal
	## origin/HEAD (missing on git < 2.47, stale after an upstream default-branch rename).
	## ssh gets a connect timeout so a dead remote can't hang every command for minutes;
	## https relies on git's own timeouts. Never clobber a user-set GIT_SSH_COMMAND.
	## No auth prompts, same as fpProbeRemote: this runs before any of our own checks, so an
	## https remote we can't authenticate to would stop and ask for a username mid-command.
	local -a fetchEnv=(GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0)
	[[ -z "${GIT_SSH_COMMAND:-}" ]] && fetchEnv+=(GIT_SSH_COMMAND="ssh -o ConnectTimeout=3")
	if env "${fetchEnv[@]}" git fetch --quiet --prune 2>/dev/null; then
		env "${fetchEnv[@]}" git remote set-head origin --auto >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
	else
		remoteReachable=0
		fEcho "WARNING: git fetch failed (offline?); remote info may be stale."
	fi
:;}

fDefaultBranch(){
	## Prefer origin's HEAD; fall back to whichever of main/master exists locally.
	[[ -n "${defaultBranchCache:-}" ]] && { echo "${defaultBranchCache}"; return 0; }
	local originHead=""
	originHead="$(git symbolic-ref --quiet --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)"
	[[ -n "${originHead}" ]] && { echo "${originHead#origin/}"; return 0; }
	git show-ref --verify --quiet refs/heads/main   && { echo "main"; return 0; }
	git show-ref --verify --quiet refs/heads/master && { echo "master"; return 0; }
	echo "main"
:;}

fMergeTarget(){
	## Feature branches come off of - and land on - dev when the repo has one; else the default branch.
	[[ -n "${mergeTargetCache:-}" ]] && { echo "${mergeTargetCache}"; return 0; }
	if fBranchExistsLocal dev || fBranchExistsRemote dev; then echo "dev"; else fDefaultBranch; fi
:;}

fIsHotfixBranch(){
	## A hotfix corrects what is already published, so it targets the default branch instead of dev.
	## The 'hotfix/' prefix is the marker: it lives in the ref name, so it survives a clone and is
	## visible in a branch listing - unlike branch-local config, which neither is.
	local -r branch="${1:-$(fCurrentBranch)}"
	[[ "${branch}" == hotfix/* ]]
}

fBranchTarget(){
	## Where THIS branch lands, which is not always where new feature branches come from.
	## fMergeTarget stays the answer for 'br create' (always dev when there is one); this is the
	## answer for landing, and for what a pull request should be based on.
	local -r branch="${1:-$(fCurrentBranch)}"
	if fIsHotfixBranch "${branch}"; then fDefaultBranch; else fMergeTarget; fi
:;}

fBackMergeRef(){
	## What the back-merge actually merges. 'pr ok' lands the hotfix on the server, so the LOCAL
	## default branch never sees it and merging that is a silent no-op; the fetched remote-tracking
	## ref is the one holding it. After 'br land' the two are the same commit, so this is right
	## either way - and falls back to the local branch when there's no remote at all.
	local -r mainBranch="$(fDefaultBranch)"
	if git rev-parse --verify --quiet "refs/remotes/origin/${mainBranch}" >/dev/null 2>&1
	then echo "origin/${mainBranch}"
	else echo "${mainBranch}"; fi
:;}

fpBackMergeToDev(){
	## After a hotfix lands on the default branch, dev has to receive it. Skipping this is how the
	## next release conflicts on the same file, or quietly reinstates the text the hotfix replaced.
	## A conflict here is raw-git territory: abort so the tree is left clean, and say so plainly.
	local -r mainBranch="$(fDefaultBranch)"
	local -r devBranch="$(fMergeTarget)"
	[[ "${devBranch}" != "${mainBranch}" ]] || return 0   ## no dev in this repo: nothing to carry back
	fBranchExistsLocal "${devBranch}" || fBranchExistsRemote "${devBranch}" || return 0
	local -r mergeRef="$(fBackMergeRef)"
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fRun git checkout "${devBranch}"
	fPullIfOnline
	fEcho "git merge ${mergeRef} ..."
	if git merge "${mergeRef}" -m "Merge ${mainBranch}"; then
		fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
		if git remote get-url origin &>/dev/null; then
			if fHasUpstream; then fRun git push; else fRun git push -u origin HEAD; fi
		fi
	else
		git merge --abort 2>/dev/null || true
		fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
		fEcho "WARNING: '${mainBranch}' would not merge cleanly into '${devBranch}'; left '${devBranch}' untouched."
		fEcho_Clean "  The hotfix landed on '${mainBranch}' - that part is done."
		fEcho_Clean "  Carry it across by hand: git checkout ${devBranch} && git merge ${mergeRef}"
	fi
:;}

fpWarnHotfixTouchedBin(){
	## A hotfix that changes shipped code leaves the default branch carrying something no tag
	## contains, so the latest release's assets stop matching it. Documentation does not.
	local -r workBranch="${1:-}"; local -r targetBranch="${2:-}"
	local changed=""
	changed="$(git diff --name-only "${targetBranch}...${workBranch}" -- bin/ 2>/dev/null || true)"
	[[ -n "${changed}" ]] || return 0
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho "NOTE: this hotfix changes shipped code, not just documentation."
	fEcho_Clean "  '${targetBranch}' will carry code that no tag contains, so the latest release's"
	fEcho_Clean "  downloads no longer match it. Cut a patch release when you're ready: ${meName} release"
:;}

fReleaseVersion(){
	## Resolve the release tag: validate the given version, or bump patch on the latest v* tag.
	local ver="${cmdArg:-}"
	ver="${ver#v}"
	if [[ -n "${ver}" ]]; then
		[[ "${ver}" =~ ^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+([-.][0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?$ ]] || fThrowError_Usage "'${ver}' is not a version (want X.Y.Z, optional -suffix). Syntax: ${meName} release [version]"
	else
		ver="0.1.0"  ## first release ever, or an unreadable tag
		local -a latestArr=()
		## versionsort.suffix=- ranks v2.0.0 above its own v2.0.0-rc1; the default sort inverts them.
		mapfile -t -n 1 latestArr < <(git -c versionsort.suffix=- tag --list 'v[0-9]*' --sort=-v:refname 2>/dev/null || true)
		if [[ "${latestArr[0]:-}" =~ ^v?([0-9]+)(\.([0-9]+))?(\.([0-9]+))?(.*)$ ]]; then
			local -r maj="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"
			local -r min="${BASH_REMATCH[3]:-0}"  ## pad short tags like v1.2 or v2020
			local pat="${BASH_REMATCH[5]:-0}"
			## A candidate's own version is what comes next: v2.0.0-rc1 -> v2.0.0, not v2.0.1.
			[[ -n "${BASH_REMATCH[6]}" ]] || pat=$(( pat + 1 ))
			ver="${maj}.${min}.${pat}"
		fi
	fi
	echo "v${ver}"
:;}

fRun(){
	## Announce and run a command verbatim - argument array, no eval, no quote games.
	## A failing command gets a plain one-line report; the trap dump stays reserved
	## for unexpected script errors.
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho "$* ..."
	local -i rc=0
	"${@}" || rc=$?
	if ((rc)); then
		fEcho_Clean ""
		echo "${meName}: '$*' failed (exit ${rc})." >&2
		fEcho_Clean_Force ""
		exit 1
	fi
	fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
:;}

fpCappedList(){
	## Indent and print a command's output, truncated to the terminal so nothing wraps and capped so a
	## huge working tree can't scroll the prompt off-screen - the 'less -FX' idea without a pager.
	local -ri maxLines=25
	local -i termWidth=100
	if [[ -t 1 ]] && command -v tput &>/dev/null; then
		termWidth="$(tput cols 2>/dev/null || echo 100)"
		((termWidth >= 40)) || termWidth=100
	fi
	local -a outLines=()
	mapfile -t outLines < <("${@}" 2>/dev/null || true)
	local -i shown=0
	local line=""
	for line in "${outLines[@]}"; do
		((shown >= maxLines)) && break
		((${#line} > termWidth - 4)) && line="${line:0:termWidth-7}..."
		fEcho_Clean "    ${line}"
		shown=$((shown + 1))
	done
	((${#outLines[@]} > shown)) && fEcho_Clean "    ... and $((${#outLines[@]} - shown)) more"
	_lastListCount=${#outLines[@]}  ## callers that need "was it empty?" read this
:;}

fpShowChanges(){
	## Short form on purpose: git status's long form buries the file list under paragraphs of hints.
	fEcho_Clean "Local changes:"
	fpCappedList git status --short
	((_lastListCount)) || fEcho_Clean "    (working tree clean)"
:;}

fpShowIncoming(){
	## The other half of "what's going to change": what a pull would bring down on top of your work.
	fHasUpstream || return 0
	local -ri behind="$(git rev-list --count 'HEAD..@{u}' 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
	((behind)) || return 0
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho_Clean "Incoming (${behind} commit(s) to pull):"
	fpCappedList git diff --name-status 'HEAD..@{u}'
:;}

fpShowIdentity(){
	## Who a remote-touching command will act as. Host aliases in ~/.ssh/config hide this, and with more
	## than one account configured it is easy to push as the wrong person.
	local -r remoteUrl="${1:-}"
	local -r sshHostAlias="$(fSshTarget "${remoteUrl}")"
	if [[ -n "${sshHostAlias}" ]] && command -v ssh &>/dev/null; then
		local sshUser="" sshHost="" keyFile="" key="" value=""
		while read -r key value; do
			case "${key}" in
				user)         [[ -n "${sshUser}" ]] || sshUser="${value}" ;;
				hostname)     [[ -n "${sshHost}" ]] || sshHost="${value}" ;;
				identityfile) [[ -n "${keyFile}" ]] || { [[ -r "${value/#\~/${HOME}}" ]] && keyFile="${value}"; } ;;
			esac
		done < <(ssh -G -- "${sshHostAlias}" 2>/dev/null || true)  ## --: an option-shaped 'host' from .git/config must not parse as an ssh option
		if [[ -n "${sshHost}" ]]; then
			local sshLine="${sshUser:-?}@${sshHost}"
			[[ "${sshHostAlias}" != "${sshHost}" ]] && sshLine="${sshLine} (alias '${sshHostAlias}')"
			[[ -n "${keyFile}" ]] && sshLine="${sshLine}, key ${keyFile}"
			fEcho_Clean "SSH ..........: ${sshLine}"
		fi
	fi
	fEcho_Clean "Author .......: $(fCommitIdentity)"
	## gh-backed commands act as gh's account, not the ssh key's - so name it where it applies.
	if ((isGhCommand)); then
		local ghLogin=""; ghLogin="$(fpGhLogin)"
		local ghLine="${ghLogin}"
		[[ "${ghLogin}" == "?" ]] && ghLine="(unknown - gh not logged in, or offline)"
		## The ssh comparison costs a round trip, so only the commands that write through gh pay it.
		if ((isGhWrite)); then
			local sshLogin=""; sshLogin="$(fpSshLogin "${identityProbeUrl}")"
			if [[ -n "$(fpIdentityMismatch "${ghLogin}" "${sshLogin}")" ]]; then
				ghLine="${ghLine}  <-- NOT the ssh key's account ('${sshLogin}')"
			elif [[ "${sshLogin}" == "?" ]]; then
				ghLine="${ghLine} (no ssh identity to compare)"
			fi
		fi
		fEcho_Clean "GitHub (gh) ..: ${ghLine}"
	fi
:;}

fpShowStatus(){
	## Arg 1: also show who we'll be on the remote - pre-flight and 'status', but not the after-shot.
	local -ri showIdentity="${1:-0}"
	local -r remoteUrl="$(git remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || echo "")"
	local -r remoteDisp="$(fMaskUrl "${remoteUrl}")"
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fEcho_Clean "Directory ....: ${PWD}"
	fEcho_Clean "Remote .......: ${remoteDisp:-(none)}"
	((showIdentity)) && fpShowIdentity "${remoteUrl}"
	local -r branchLine="$(fCurrentBranch) (repo default: $(fDefaultBranch)) $(fBranchSync)"
	fEcho_Clean "Branch .......: ${branchLine% }"
	fEcho_Clean ""
	fpShowChanges
	((showIdentity)) && fpShowIncoming
	fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
:;}

fpPreview(){
	## Static per-command recipe; the command functions do the real state checks at run time.
	## 'commit' and 'pull' are no longer commands of their own - they stay here as the
	## fragments update/sync/br-* compose their own plans from.
	local -r pad="    "
	local msgDisp="git commit"
	[[ -n "${commitMessage}" ]] && msgDisp="git commit -m \"${commitMessage}\""
	case "${1:-}" in
		commit)
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git add --all"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}${msgDisp} *"
			;;
		pull)
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only --autostash *"
			;;
		update)
			fpPreview pull
			fpPreview commit
			;;
		sync)
			fpPreview update
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push (branch '$(fCurrentBranch)') *"
			;;
		br-create)
			## From main/dev the dirty tree rides along to the new branch, so there's no commit here.
			if fIsProtectedBranch; then
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fMergeTarget) *"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only --autostash *"
			else
				fpPreview sync
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fMergeTarget) *"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only *"
			fi
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout -b ${cmdArg}"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push -u origin ${cmdArg} *"
			;;
		br-hotfix)
			## Off the default branch, not dev: this corrects what is already published.
			if fIsProtectedBranch; then
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fDefaultBranch) *"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only --autostash *"
			else
				fpPreview sync
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fDefaultBranch) *"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only *"
			fi
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout -b ${cmdArg}"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push -u origin ${cmdArg} *"
			;;
		br-switch)
			fpPreview sync
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout ${cmdArg:-$(fMergeTarget)}"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only *"
			;;
		br-land)
			fpPreview sync
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fBranchTarget)"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only *"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git merge --no-ff $(fCurrentBranch)"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push *"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git branch -d $(fCurrentBranch)"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push origin --delete $(fCurrentBranch) *"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only *"
			## A hotfix owes dev the same change, or the next release undoes it.
			if fIsHotfixBranch; then
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fMergeTarget)"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git merge $(fBackMergeRef)"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push *"
			fi
			;;
		br-prune)
			local pruneBranch=""
			## -D is what runs, so -D is what the plan says. The line above it is the reason
			## that's safe: gitsby checked containment itself, against the branch that matters.
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}(each verified contained in $(fMergeTarget), and re-checked at delete time)"
			for pruneBranch in "${pruneLocal[@]}";  do fEcho_Clean "${pad}git branch -D ${pruneBranch}"; done
			for pruneBranch in "${pruneRemote[@]}"; do fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push origin --delete ${pruneBranch}"; done
			[[ -n "${pruneCurrentMerged}" ]] && fEcho_Clean "${pad}Keeping '${pruneCurrentMerged}' - merged, but it's the current branch."
			((${#pruneKeep[@]})) && fEcho_Clean "${pad}Keeping (not merged yet): $(fJoin "${pruneKeep[@]}")"
			;;
		repo-clone)
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git clone $(fMaskUrl "${cloneUrl}") ${cloneDir}"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git -C ${cloneDir} checkout dev *"
			;;
		repo-create|repo-connect)
			((inRepo)) || fEcho_Clean "${pad}git init -b main"
			fpPreview commit
			case "${connectMode}" in
				create)
					fEcho_Clean "${pad}gh repo create ${ghTarget} --${repoVisibility} --source . --push --remote origin"
					;;
				add)
					fEcho_Clean "${pad}git remote add origin $(fMaskUrl "${connectUrl}")"
					fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push -u origin HEAD"
					;;
				push)
					fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push -u origin HEAD *"
					;;
			esac
			;;
		pr)
			if [[ "${prSub}" == "create" ]]; then
				fpPreview sync
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}gh pr create --base $(fBranchTarget) --title \"${prTitle}\""
			else
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}gh pr review ${prNum} --approve *"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}gh pr merge ${prNum} --merge --delete-branch"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fBranchTarget "${prHeadBranch}") *"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only *"
				if fIsHotfixBranch "${prHeadBranch}"; then
					fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fMergeTarget)"
					fEcho_Clean "${pad}git merge $(fBackMergeRef)"
					fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push *"
				fi
			fi
			;;
		release)
			fpPreview sync
			if [[ "$(fMergeTarget)" == "dev" ]]; then
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout dev *"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only *"
			fi
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fDefaultBranch) *"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git pull --ff-only *"
			[[ "$(fMergeTarget)" == "dev" ]] && fEcho_Clean "${pad}git merge --no-ff dev"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git tag -a ${releaseTag}"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push *"
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push origin ${releaseTag} *"
			if [[ "$(fMergeTarget)" == "dev" ]]; then
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout dev *"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git merge --ff-only $(fDefaultBranch) *"
				fEcho_Clean "${pad}git push *"
			fi
			fEcho_Clean "${pad}git checkout $(fCurrentBranch) *"
			;;
	esac
:;}


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
fParseArgs(){

	## Look for stars "✶✶✶✶✶✶✶✶" for places to custom-modify unique instances of this function.

	## GENERIC (don't modify): Populate args array and string.
	## Note: Args are 1-based index, but the resulting array of args at the end is a normal 0-based index.
	local -r -i maxPracticalArgCount=50  ## In Bash we have 2MB of args, or somewhere around 10k by count. For readabality and performance, keep it MUCH lower, say ~20-99.
	local    -i totalArgCount=0
	local    -i switchCounter=0
	local -a    allArgsArr=()
	## Store the highest non-empty argument number.
	local -i i; i=0
	for ((i=1; i<=maxPracticalArgCount; i++)); do [[ -n "${!i:-}" ]] && totalArgCount=$i; done
	local -ri totalArgCount=$totalArgCount
	## Build args array, including empty args - up to the last non-empty arg.
	## Args pass through untouched (no eval anywhere downstream, so quotes are safe as-is).
	for ((i=1; i<=totalArgCount; i++)); do
		allArgsArr+=("${!i}")
	done

	## GENERIC: Variables for loop
	local    currentArg=""
	local    lastSwitch=""
	local    lastSwitchAsPassed=""
	local    tmpStr=""
	local -i expectingSwitchParamForNextArg=0
	local -i positionalArgCounter=0
	local -r switchRegex='^--?[^ -]'

	## Process arguments
	for currentArg in "${allArgsArr[@]}"; do

		## Test if an option switch or not
		if [[ "${currentArg}" =~ ${switchRegex} ]]; then
			## It's an option (either unary or long-option, we don't know yet)

			## Check if this was supposed to NOT be a unary switch (eg expecting a parameter after previous long-option)
			((expectingSwitchParamForNextArg)) && fThrowError "Expecting a long-option argument for '${lastSwitch}', instead got another option '${currentArg}'."
			switchCounter=$((switchCounter + 1))

			## Validate switches, and act on unary switches
			lastSwitchAsPassed="${currentArg}"
			tmpStr="${currentArg,,}"

			## Strip switch dashes off
			while [[ "${tmpStr}" == -* ]]; do tmpStr="${tmpStr#-}"; done
			lastSwitch="${tmpStr}" ## Remember lastSwitch

			case "${tmpStr}" in

			## ✶✶✶✶✶✶✶✶ CUSTOM UNARY SWITCHES
				"q"|"quiet"|"y"|"yes")      doQuietly=1 ;;
				"no-fetch"|"nofetch"|"offline")  doFetch=0 ;;
				"public")                   repoVisibility="public" ;;
				"private")                  repoVisibility="private" ;;
				"any-identity"|"anyidentity")  anyIdentity=1 ;;
				"m="*|"message="*|"msg="*)  commitMessage="${currentArg#*=}" ;;  ## -m=MSG / --message=MSG / --msg=MSG (joined)

			## ✶✶✶✶✶✶✶✶ CUSTOM LONG-OPTIONS THAT EXPECT AN ARGUMENT TO FOLLOW
				"m"|"message"|"msg")        expectingSwitchParamForNextArg=1 ;;  ## -m MSG (spaced; value captured below)

				## ¯\_(ツ)_/¯
				*) fThrowError "Unexpected option in this context: '${currentArg}'." ;;

			esac
		else
			## It's not an option switch; could be a long-option argument, or a positional arg

			if ((expectingSwitchParamForNextArg)); then
				## Now we know we're expecting a long-option argument

			## ✶✶✶✶✶✶✶✶ CUSTOM LONG-OPTION ARGUMENT TESTS AND PARENT VARIABLE ASSIGNMENTS
				case "${lastSwitch,,}" in
					"m"|"message"|"msg") commitMessage="${currentArg}" ;;
					*)                   fThrowError "Unknown option: '${lastSwitchAsPassed}', current parameter: '${currentArg}'." ;;
				esac
				expectingSwitchParamForNextArg=0

			else
				## Well it must be a positional arg then.
				positionalArgCounter=$((positionalArgCounter + 1))
				((positionalArgCounter > parseArgs_maxPositionalArgCount)) && fThrowError "Too many positional arguments: ${positionalArgCounter}, for max of ${parseArgs_maxPositionalArgCount}."

				## ✶✶✶✶✶✶✶✶ CUSTOM POSITIONAL ARG LOGIC (assigns parent variables)
				case $positionalArgCounter in
					1) cmdName="${currentArg}" ;;
					2) cmdArg="${currentArg}"  ;;
					3) cmdArg2="${currentArg}" ;;
					4) cmdArg3="${currentArg}" ;;
					*) fThrowError "Unexpected positional argument # ${positionalArgCounter}: '${currentArg}'." ;;
				esac

			fi
		fi
	done
	((expectingSwitchParamForNextArg)) && fThrowError "Never received a parameter for switch '--${lastSwitch}'."

:;}  ## 'true' has to be the last thing or this function errors [the joys of the mysterious 'set -e'].


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Generic functions

fMustBeInPath(){
	local -r programToCheckForInPath="${1:-}"
	if [[ -z "${programToCheckForInPath}" ]]; then
		fThrowError "No program specified."; return 1
	elif ! command -v "${programToCheckForInPath}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then  ## builtin; 'which' is absent on some minimal distros
		fThrowError_Usage "Not found in path: ${programToCheckForInPath}"; return 1
	fi ;:;}
fPromptYN(){
	((doQuietly)) && return 0
	local promptStr="${1:-}"
	[[ -z "${promptStr}" ]] && promptStr="Continue? (y|n): "
	read -r -p "${promptStr}" userAnswer
	fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
	{ [[ "${userAnswer,,}" == "y" ]] && return 0; } || return 1; }

## Echo-related
declare -i _lastListCount=0  ## set by fpCappedList()
declare -i _wasLastEchoBlank=0
fEcho_ResetBlankCounter() { _wasLastEchoBlank=0; }
fEcho_Clean()             { if [[ -n "${1:-}" ]]; then printf '%s\n' "$*"; _wasLastEchoBlank=0; elif [[ $_wasLastEchoBlank -eq 0 ]] && echo; then _wasLastEchoBlank=1; fi; }  ## printf, not echo -e: never re-animate escape bytes from user data
fEcho()                   { if [[ -n "$*" ]]; then fEcho_Clean "[ $* ]"; else fEcho_Clean ""; fi; }
fEcho_Force()             { fEcho_ResetBlankCounter; fEcho "$*";       }
fEcho_Clean_Force()       { fEcho_ResetBlankCounter; fEcho_Clean "$*"; }

## Error-handling
declare -i _wasCleanupRun=0  ## Managed internally by this suite.
declare -i _doExitOnThrow=0  ## Managed internally by this suite.
declare -i _ErrVal=0         ## Set by this suite, but managed by calling functions. Think of it as an extended '$?' that doesn't immediately clear.
fCleanup(){ fEcho_Clean; }
_fSingleExitPoint(){
	local -r signal="${1:-}"
	local -r lineNum="${2:-}"
	local -r exitCode="${3:-}"
	local -r errMsg="${4:-}"
	local -r errCommand="$BASH_COMMAND"
	_ErrVal=$exitCode
	if [[ "${signal}" == "INT" ]]; then
		fEcho_Force
		echo "User interrupted." >&2
		fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
		fCleanup  ## User cleanup
		exit 1
	elif [[ "${exitCode}" != "0" ]] && [[ "${exitCode}" != "1" ]]; then  ## Clunky string compare is less likely to fail than integer
		fEcho_Clean
		echo -e "Signal .....: '${signal}'"      >&2
		echo -e "Err# .......: '${exitCode}'"    >&2
		echo -e "Message ....: '${errMsg}'"      >&2
		echo -e "At line# ...: '${lineNum}'"     >&2
		echo -e "Command# ...: '${errCommand}'"  >&2
		fEcho_Clean_Force
		fCleanup  ## User cleanup
	else
		fCleanup  ## User cleanup
	fi ;}
_fTrap_Exit(){
	if [[ "${_wasCleanupRun}" == "0" ]]; then  ## String compare is less likely to fail than integer
		_wasCleanupRun=1
		_fSingleExitPoint "${@}"
	fi ;}
_fTrap_Error(){
	if [[ "${_wasCleanupRun}" == "0" ]]; then  ## String compare is less likely to fail than integer
		_wasCleanupRun=1
		fEcho_ResetBlankCounter
		_fSingleExitPoint "${@}"
	fi ;}
_fTrap_Error_Ignore(){ _ErrVal=1; true;  return 0; }
_fTrap_Error_Soft(){   _ErrVal=1; false; return 1; }
fThrowError(){
	local errMsg="${1:-}"         ; [[ -z "${errMsg}"      ]] && errMsg="An error occurred."
	local meNameLocal="${meName}" ; [[ -n "${meNameLocal}" ]] && errMsg="${meNameLocal}: ${errMsg}"
	local callStack=""
	if [[ -z "${_throwNoStack:-}" ]]; then  ## usage errors (fThrowError_Usage) skip the stack noise
		for (( i = 1; i < ${#FUNCNAME[@]}; i++ )); do
			[[ "${FUNCNAME[i]}" =~ main|source|fThrowError_Usage ]] && continue
			[[ -n "${callStack}" ]] && callStack="${callStack}, "; callStack="${callStack}${FUNCNAME[i]}()"
		done
		[[ -n "${callStack}" ]] && callStack="Reverse call stack: ${callStack}"
	fi
	fEcho_Clean
	{ printf '%s\n' "${errMsg}"; if [[ -n "${callStack}" ]]; then printf '%s\n' "${callStack}"; fi; } >&2
	fEcho_Clean_Force
	_ErrVal=1
	{ ((_doExitOnThrow)) && exit 1; } || return 1; }
fThrowError_Usage(){
	## Same as fThrowError, minus the call-stack line - expected validation errors aren't crashes.
	local -r _throwNoStack=1
	fThrowError "${@}"; }
fDefineTrap_Error_Fatal(){        :; _ErrVal=0; _doExitOnThrow=0; trap '_fTrap_Error         ERR    ${LINENO}  $?  $_' ERR; set -e; } ## Standard; exits script on any caught error; but 'set -e' has known inconsistencies catching or ignoring errors.
fDefineTrap_Error_ExitOnThrow(){  :; _ErrVal=0; _doExitOnThrow=0; trap '_fTrap_Error         ERR    ${LINENO}  $?  $_' ERR; set +e; } ## Only exits script on fThrowError().
fDefineTrap_Error_Soft(){         :; _ErrVal=0; _doExitOnThrow=0; trap '_fTrap_Error_Soft    ERR    ${LINENO}  $?  $_' ERR; set -e; } ## Returns error code of 1 on error.
fDefineTrap_Error_Ignore(){       :; _ErrVal=0; _doExitOnThrow=0; trap '_fTrap_Error_Ignore  ERR    ${LINENO}  $?  $_' ERR; set +e; } ## Eats errors and returns true.
fDefineTrap_Error_Fatal
trap '_fTrap_Error SIGHUP  ${LINENO} $? $_' SIGHUP
trap '_fTrap_Error SIGINT  ${LINENO} $? $_' SIGINT    ## CTRL+C
trap '_fTrap_Error SIGTERM ${LINENO} $? $_' SIGTERM
trap '_fTrap_Exit  EXIT    ${LINENO} $? $_' EXIT
trap '_fTrap_Exit  INT     ${LINENO} $? $_' INT
trap '_fTrap_Exit  TERM    ${LINENO} $? $_' TERM


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Script entry point

## Bash environment settings (comment out what you don't want)
 set -u  #..................: Require variable declaration. Stronger than mere linting. But can struggle if functions are in sourced files.
 set -e  #..................: Exit on errors. This is inconsistent (made a little better with settings below), so eventually may move to 'set +e' (which is more constant work and mental overhead).
 set -E  #..................: Propagate ERR trap settings into functions, command substitutions, and subshells.
 set   -o pipefail  #.......: Make sure all stages of piped commands also fail the same.
 shopt -s inherit_errexit  #: Propagate 'set -e' ........ into functions, command substitutions, and subshells. Will fail on Bash <4.4.

## Check if sourced
declare -i isSourced; { (return 0 2>/dev/null) && isSourced=1; } || isSourced=0

## Common constants but detect if already set
[[ -v serialDT ]] || declare -r serialDT="$(date "+%Y%m%d-%H%M%S")"
[[ -v mePath   ]] || declare -r mePath="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
[[ -v meName   ]] || declare -r meName="$(basename "${mePath}")"
[[ -v meDir    ]] || declare -r meDir="$(cd "$(dirname "${mePath}")" && pwd)"

## Invoke main (not when sourced, e.g. by the test harness)
((isSourced)) || fMain "${@}"


